# Security rules

## Context and trust boundary
- This application is an internal company AI knowledge base and chat. No anonymous user may access the application, API, documents, search results, chat history, or streamed responses.
- Treat every request, user message, uploaded file, retrieved document, tool response, and model output as untrusted. Never treat document text or model output as an instruction to change access controls or execute privileged actions.
- Default to deny. Authentication answers who a user is; authorization answers which workspace, document, conversation, or action that user may access. Enforce both on the server for every request.

## Authentication and authorization
- Use company SSO/OIDC with approved accounts and an explicit employee/group allowlist. Do not rely on email domain alone. Disable self-service signup, guest access, and public sharing.
- Protect all application pages and API endpoints, including App Router route handlers, server actions, uploads, search, chat streaming, document downloads, webhooks, and background-job entry points. Middleware may redirect unauthenticated users but is not the sole security boundary.
- Re-check the session and authorization inside each server-side handler/action. Reject unauthenticated requests with 401 and unauthorized requests with 403. Never trust client-provided role, userId, tenantId, department, conversationId, or documentId.
- Apply least-privilege roles (for example: employee, content manager, admin). Verify resource ownership and document permissions on every read/write; scope database queries to the authorized organization and user/group.
- Enforce access controls before retrieval and again before returning citations, snippets, files, or streamed tokens. Filter vector search by authorized scope; do not retrieve globally and only filter in the UI.
- Derive user/organization context from the validated server session, not request body or query parameters. Admin routes must explicitly check the admin role.
- Set secure, HttpOnly, SameSite session cookies in production; use HTTPS. Follow the chosen auth provider's CSRF/state/PKCE requirements and prevent open redirects.

## Private deployment and network
- Require identity-aware access at the ingress (company VPN, private network, or SSO access gateway) in addition to app-level authentication. Do not expose the deployment URL as an anonymous service.
- Put databases, object storage, vector stores, and model gateways on private networks or restrictive allowlists where possible. Limit outbound access and disable unnecessary external integrations.
- Disable public registration, public API tokens, public buckets, unauthenticated preview deployments, and public search indexing. Treat robots.txt and noindex as supplemental only, never as access controls.
- Restrict CI previews to authenticated company members; do not use production data in preview or staging.

## Secrets and data protection
- Keep API keys, auth secrets, database credentials, and encryption keys server-side in environment/secrets management. Never use NEXT_PUBLIC_ for secrets or import server-only modules into client components.
- Do not commit .env files, credentials, private documents, embeddings, chat logs, or production database dumps. Rotate exposed secrets immediately.
- Use TLS in transit and encryption at rest where supported. Set retention periods for uploads, chats, logs, and backups; ensure backups follow the same access policy.
- Log security-relevant events with actor, action, resource, time, and result, but redact tokens, passwords, prompts, document bodies, model outputs, and sensitive personal/company data.
- Do not send internal knowledge to an external AI/model/analytics provider unless the company has approved the provider, data processing terms, region, retention policy, and training/zero-retention settings.

## AI chat and retrieval
- Ingest only approved sources. Validate file type, size, and content server-side; scan uploads as required by company policy. Use non-executable parsing and sanitize content before rendering.
- Store document provenance and access metadata with each chunk and embedding. Reindex or revoke promptly when permissions change; deny access if metadata is missing or stale.
- Keep system/developer instructions separate from retrieved text. Instruct the model to treat retrieved content as data, not commands; do not allow retrieved content to override policies, call privileged tools, or expose hidden prompts/secrets.
- For any tool/action the AI can invoke, enforce independent server-side authorization, strict schemas, allowlists, scoped credentials, rate limits, and explicit user approval for sensitive writes. Never let the model choose arbitrary URLs, SQL, shell commands, or file paths.
- Validate citations and source permissions before display. Do not let the model cite or reveal a document the requesting user cannot open.
- Limit input length, upload size, token usage, request frequency, concurrency, and cost per user. Handle abuse and prompt injection without leaking sensitive details in errors.

## Web and API hardening
- Validate all inputs on the server using schemas; use parameterized queries and safe ORM methods. Escape rendered text, sanitize any allowed HTML/Markdown, and never render raw model output with dangerouslySetInnerHTML.
- Use restrictive Content Security Policy and appropriate security headers. Restrict CORS to approved origins; CORS is not authentication.
- Guard state-changing cookie-authenticated requests against CSRF; avoid GET for mutations. Verify webhook signatures and reject replayed requests when supported.
- Return generic public error messages and keep detailed traces in restricted server logs. Do not leak stack traces, internal URLs, provider metadata, or document existence to unauthorized callers.
- Pin and audit dependencies; address high/critical findings before release. Never disable lint, type checks, or security tests merely to pass a build.

## Review checklist
- For every new page, route, server action, query, upload, tool, or integration, document the principal, resource, authorization check, data classification, and failure behavior.
- If security requirements conflict with a feature request, stop and propose a safe implementation rather than silently weakening a guardrail.